  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Goalkeeper – the right position when possession changes",
  "description": "A five-a-side game, four outfield players and a keeper per team, on a small pitch about 30 metres long and 25 metres wide, with full-size goals. On the small pitch the ball is almost always close to one of the goals, and possession changes often.\n\nThe keeper works on their position in both phases. When the opponents have the ball, they move with it, stay on the line between the ball and the centre of the goal, and come out as far as they can without being chipped. When their own team has the ball, they step up and to the side so they are always available for a back pass and can reach balls played in behind the defenders. The coach can stop play now and then: everyone freezes, and the keepers say for themselves whether they are in the right place.\n\nPlay four periods of four minutes. With more players, make the pitch wider rather than longer, so there is more play from the wings. Play offside if the team does in matches. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "The ball moves – you move. Small, quick steps, and be still when the shot comes."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Opponents’ ball: on the line between the ball and the centre of the goal, and as far out as the situation allows."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Own team’s ball: step up towards the edge of the box and show yourself for a back pass."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Talk all the time and tell the defenders where to stand."
    }
  ],
